verses.text:
I will turn my hand against you.
I will melt away your waste as with strong soap,
and I will remove all your impure metal.{k}

You have abandoned your people, the family of Jacob.
They are loaded with beliefs imported from the east.
They read signs like the Philistines,{d}
and they shake hands on deals with outsiders.{e}

Their land is full of idols.
They bow down to things their own hands made,
things their own fingers shaped.{f}

So people are bent low,
and everyone is brought down.
Do not forgive them!{g}

Quit putting your faith in mortals,
who live on one breath through their nostrils.
Of what value are they?{l}

Jerusalem has tripped, and Judah has fallen.
Their words and their actions go against Yahweh
and spit in the face of his glory.{c}

How horrible for the wicked!
Disaster is coming,
because they will be paid back for what their hands did.{d}

When that day comes, the Branch of Yahweh will be lovely and full of glory.
The land’s harvest will be the pride and delight
of Israel’s survivors.{a}

I heard Yahweh of Armies swear this:
“Many houses will end up empty,
big and beautiful ones with nobody living in them.{d}

They called out to each other:
“Holy, holy, holy is Yahweh of Armies!
His glory fills all the earth!”{c}

This is what the Lord Yahweh says:{e}
‘That plan will fail.
It will never come true.

“These people turned down
the gently flowing water of Shiloah.{d}
They are delighted with Rezin and Remaliah’s son.

Make your plans, and they will fail.
Give your orders, and they will not stand,
because God is with us.{g}

Yahweh of Armies is furious, and the land burns.
The people become firewood.
Nobody has mercy on his brother.{l}

One man carves meat on the right and stays hungry.
Another eats on the left and is never full.
Each one chews on his own arm.{m}

Calno fell just like Carchemish.
Hamath fell just like Arpad.
Samaria fell just like Damascus.{d}

It will wipe out the splendor of his forests and orchards,
from top to bottom,
like a sick man wasting away.{i}

The enemy has reached Aiath.
He has moved through Migron.
At Michmash he leaves his supplies.{p}

Scream at the top of your voice, Gallim!
Listen, Laishah!
Poor Anathoth, answer her!{r}

Today the enemy will pause at Nob.
He will wave his fist at Mount Zion,
the hill where Jerusalem sits.{t}

I have given orders to the troops I set apart.
I have summoned my warriors to carry out my anger,
my proud and happy fighters.{b}

Their babies will be smashed as they watch.
Their homes will be stripped bare,
and their wives will be raped.{g}

I am stirring up the Medes to come against Babylon.
Silver means nothing to them,
and gold holds no charm.{h}

When that day comes, you will recite this mocking poem about Babylon’s king:
“The bully is finished!
His wild rage is over!{c}

The waters of Nimrim are dried up.
The grass has withered, the young plants are gone,
and nothing green is left.{e}

So they carry off the wealth they earned
and the goods they stored up
across the Willow Brook.{f}

The cry has gone all around Moab’s border.
The wailing reaches Eglaim,
and the wailing reaches Beer-elim.{g}

Send young sheep as a gift to the ruler of the land.
Send them from Sela, across the desert,
to the mountain of Zion.{a}

This is a message about Damascus.{a}
Damascus will stop being a city.
It will turn into a pile of rubble.

The towns of Aroer will be empty.{b}
Flocks will lie down there,
and no one will scare them.

The canals will smell bad.
Egypt’s streams will shrink and dry out,
and the reeds and rushes will wither.{d}

The grassy banks along the Nile, at the river’s mouth,
and every field planted beside the Nile
will dry up, blow away, and be gone.{e}

Nothing will go right in Egypt
for head or tail,
palm branch or reed.{j}

My mind is spinning. Horror has swamped me.
The evening I longed for
has turned into shaking and shivering.{c}

The sentry shouted like a lion:{f}
“Lord, all day long I stand on the tower,
and every night I stay on guard.

A word about Arabia.
You Dedanite caravans,
you spend your nights in the brush of Arabia.{j}

Carry water out to the thirsty.
People of Tema,
greet the refugees with food.{k}

They fled from swords,
from the sword pulled from its sheath,
from the bow pulled tight,
and from the thick of the fight.

A word about the Valley of Vision.{a}
What has come over you,
that every one of you has gone up to the roofs,

Elam picked up the quiver,
with chariots, riders, and horses.
Kir took the cover off the shield.{d}

The enemy tore down Judah’s defenses.
On that day you looked
to the weapons in the House of the Forest.{e}

David’s family key will rest on his shoulder.
Whatever he opens will stay open,
and whatever he locks will stay locked.{j}

Spread over your land like the Nile,
daughter of Tarshish.
Nothing holds you back now.{d}

Look! Yahweh is about to strip the earth bare and ruin it.
He will twist its surface
and scatter those who live there.{a}

They will be gathered in a heap like captives in a hole,
locked away in a dungeon.
After a long time they will be called to account.{i}

Yahweh, you are my God.
I will raise you up and thank you by name.
The wonders you have done are plans from long ago, faithful and true.{a}

On this mountain he will swallow up
the cover that hangs over all peoples
and the veil that is spread over all nations.{c}

Yahweh’s hand will rest on this mountain,
but Moab will be trampled where it stands,
like straw trampled into a manure pit.{f}

On that day people in Judah will sing this song:
“We have a strong city.
God uses rescue as its walls and its outer wall.{a}

You guard the mind that leans on you
with perfect peace,
because that person trusts you.{c}

Yahweh, your hand is lifted, but they will not notice.
Make them see how fiercely you care for your people, and shame them.
Let the fire you stored up for your foes eat them.{g}

Yahweh, in trouble they looked for you.
They whispered a prayer
when your punishment was on them.{i}

It is ‘tsav latsav, tsav latsav,
qav laqav, qav laqav,
a little here, a little there.’”{c}

Yahweh will stand up as he did at Mount Perazim.
He will stir as he did in the valley of Gibeon,
for a job that seems strange to everyone,
a task that seems foreign.{h}

This also comes from Yahweh of Armies.
His plans are wonderful,
and his wisdom is great.{l}

Still, I will make life hard for Ariel.
There will be moaning and mourning,
and she will be to me like an altar fire.{b}

I will camp all around you.
I will surround you with watchtowers
and set up siege ramps against you.{c}

But the crowd of your enemies will be like fine dust,
and the pack of ruthless attackers like chaff blowing by.
It will happen suddenly, in a flash.{d}

All the nations that fight Ariel,
everyone who attacks her and her fortress and squeezes her tight,
will turn out to be a dream, a vision in the night.{e}

Yahweh has poured a spirit of deep sleep over you.
He has closed the eyes of you prophets
and covered the heads of you seers.{g}

In just a little while
Lebanon will turn into a fruit orchard,
and the orchard will seem like a forest.{k}

They make a person guilty with a single word.
They set traps for the one who speaks up at the city gate.
They push the innocent away from justice with empty charges.{l}

Egypt’s help is empty and useless.
So I have named her
“Rahab Who Sits Still.”{d}

Yahweh, show us kindness. We have waited for you.
Be our strength each morning
and our rescue in hard times.{b}

He will make your times steady.
He will fill your storehouse with rescue, wisdom, and knowledge.
Respect for Yahweh is the treasure.{c}

Your mind will go back to the fear and wonder,
“Where did the officer who counted go? Where is the man who weighed out the payment?
Where is the one who tallied the towers?”{g}

My sword has soaked up its fill in the sky.
Now see it fall on Edom,
on the people I have marked out for judgment.{c}

I dug wells and drank water.
My feet alone dried up
every Egyptian stream.’{l}

I said, “Right in the prime of my life
I have to go through the gates of the grave.
My remaining years are being taken from me.”{e}

I cried out until dawn.
He crushes all my bones like a lion.
Between morning and night you finish me off.{h}

Lord, people live because of such things,
and in all of them my spirit finds life.
Heal me and keep me alive!{k}

Then Yahweh’s glory will appear,
and everyone alive will see it together.
Yahweh has spoken it with his own mouth.”{e}

The grass dries up and the flowers fade
when Yahweh’s breath blows on them.
The people really are grass.{g}

See, the nations are like a drop from a pail.
They count as the film of dust on a scale.
He picks up the islands like grains of powder.{m}

An idol? A craftsman casts it,
a goldsmith covers it with a thin layer of gold
and fits it with silver chains.{o}

The island peoples saw it and were afraid.
The ends of the earth shook.
They drew near and came.{e}

But you, Israel, are my servant.
Jacob, you are the one I selected,
descendants of Abraham, my friend.{f}

Look, you amount to nothing,
and what you do is worthless.
Anyone who chooses you is vile.{n}

See, they are all fakes.
Their works are nothing.
Their metal statues are wind and emptiness.”{q}

He will not snap a bent reed,
and he will not put out a wick that barely burns.
He will bring justice out of faithfulness.{b}

He will not grow dim or be crushed
until he has set justice on the earth.
Far-off islands wait for his teaching.”{c}

From today on I am the one.
No one can take anything out of my hand.
When I act, who can undo it?”{c}

I, I myself, am the one
who wipes out your rebellion, for my own sake.
I will not recall your sins.{h}

So I will disgrace the priests who lead the holy place.
I will give Jacob over to destruction
and Israel to insults.”{j}

This is what Yahweh says, Israel’s King and Redeemer, Yahweh of Armies:
“I come first, and I come last.
No other God exists but me.{d}

They have no knowledge and no understanding.
Their eyes are smeared over so they cannot see,
and their minds are shut so they cannot grasp anything.{j}

He is eating ashes. His fooled mind has misled him.
He cannot rescue himself or ask,
“Is the thing in my right hand not a lie?”{l}

I form the light and make the dark.
I bring well-being and send disaster.
I, Yahweh, do all these things.”{d}

They crouch and bow down together.
They could not save the load,
and they themselves have gone off into captivity.{b}

You said, ‘I will always be queen!’
You did not take these things to heart
or think about how it would all end.{d}

For my own sake, for my own sake, I act.
How could my name be dishonored?
I will not hand my glory to another.{d}

They did not go thirsty when he led them through dry lands.
He made water flow for them from the rock.
He split the rock, and water poured out.{j}

Now Yahweh speaks,
who shaped me in the womb to serve him,
to bring Jacob back to him
and gather Israel around him,
for I am honored in Yahweh’s eyes,
and my God is my strength:{e}

Look, some will come from far away,
some from the north and the west,
and some from the land of Sinim.”{j}

The one who clears my name is near.
Who will bring charges against me? Let us stand up together.
Who is my opponent? Let him step up to me.{f}

“I am the very one who gives you comfort.
Who are you to fear a human who will die,
a person made like grass,{k}

Soon the one bent over in chains will be freed.
He will not die in the pit,
and he will not run out of bread.{l}

For I am Yahweh your God,
who stirs the sea until its waves roar.
Yahweh of Armies is his name.{m}

“See, my servant will do well.{e}
He will be raised up, lifted high,
and greatly honored.

“Poor city, beaten by storms and not comforted,
look, I will set your stones in bright mortar{c}
and build your foundations with sapphires.{d}

I will build your towers from rubies,
your gates from sparkling stones,
and your whole wall from gems.{e}

Israel’s lookouts are blind.{c}
None of them knows a thing.
Every one is a mute guard dog
that cannot bark.
They sprawl on the ground, dreaming,{d}
and love to snooze.

They go into peace.
Those who lived honestly
now rest on their beds.{a}

The Spirit of my Lord Yahweh rests on me,{a}
because Yahweh has chosen me.
He sent me to tell good news to the poor,
to bandage the brokenhearted,
to announce freedom to captives,
and to open the prison for those in chains.{b}

He sent me to announce the year of Yahweh’s favor,
and the day when our God pays back,{c}
and to comfort everyone who grieves.

People will not call you Left Behind{b}
or your land Empty{c} any more.
Your name will be My Delight Is in Her,{d}
and your land will be called Married,{e}
because Yahweh enjoys you,
and your land will have a husband.

Since the earliest times nobody has heard or noticed,
and no eye has seen any God but you
acting for the person who waits for him.{c}

Yet now, Yahweh, you are our Father.
We are the clay, and you are the potter.
All of us are made by your hand.{g}

Sharon’s plain will turn into pasture for sheep,
and the Valley of Achor into a resting spot for herds,{f}
for my people who looked for me.

Yahweh will judge the whole earth
with fire and with his sword,
and many will be killed by Yahweh.{e}

“I know what they do and what they think.{g}
I will gather every nation and language to me.
They will arrive and see my glory.
